@startuml observability
title Observability - three questions, three paths, one traceId

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center

actor "operator" as OP

node "individuals-api" as API {
  component "AuthMetrics\nsix counters, two timers" as METERS
  component "RequestLogFilter\nJSON to stdout" as LOGS
  component "OpenTelemetry\nspans" as SPANS
}

node "prometheus" as PROM
node "alloy" as ALLOY
database "loki" as LOKI
database "tempo" as TEMPO
node "grafana" as GRAF

' --- metrics: pulled ---
PROM -[#0b6e4f,bold]-> METERS : **scrape** every 15s\nGET /actuator/prometheus

' --- logs: written, then collected ---
LOGS -[#b45309,bold]-> ALLOY : stdout\nread through the Docker socket
ALLOY -[#b45309,bold]-> LOKI : push

' --- traces: pushed by the application itself ---
SPANS -[#6d28d9,bold]-> TEMPO : **push** OTLP\ntempo:4318/v1/traces

GRAF --> PROM
GRAF --> LOKI
GRAF --> TEMPO
OP --> GRAF

note right of PROM
  **How many, and how fast?**

  Numbers over time. The application
  keeps no history - it only knows
  "right now it is 4". Prometheus
  walks over and writes it down.

  Ask **rate(...)**, never the raw
  value: a counter resets to zero on
  restart, and rate knows that.
end note

note right of ALLOY
  **What actually happened?**

  The app writes JSON to stdout and
  nothing more. Docker keeps that in a
  file that dies with the container, so
  an agent has to read it and ship it.

  Alloy turns Docker metadata into Loki
  labels - service, container, env - and
  lifts level, traceId and spanId out of
  the JSON body.
end note

note right of TEMPO
  **Where did the request go, and
  where did it get stuck?**

  One traceId per request, one spanId
  per operation inside it. A parent id
  on each span is what builds the tree.

  This is the only place that shows one
  login costing seven calls outwards.
end note

note bottom of GRAF
  **Prometheus pulls. The application pushes.**
  That is why the app's address lives in prometheus.yml
  while Tempo's address lives in application.yml.
  One arrow points each way, and mixing them up is
  the usual reason a panel stays empty.
end note

note bottom of LOKI
  **traceId ties a log line to its trace.**
  It is in both, so one click in Grafana goes from
  a line to the whole request.

  Metrics carry no traceId and cannot: a counter is
  "how many in total", with no individual requests
  inside it. Metrics say **that** something is wrong;
  logs and traces say **what**.
end note

@enduml


@startuml trace-tree
title One trace, many spans - what Tempo draws for a registration

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam rectangleBorderColor #6d28d9

rectangle "**trace** 16bc50fe...  one id for the whole request" as T {
  rectangle "**server span**\nPOST /api/v1/auth/registration\nparent: none - this one starts the trace" as S0

  rectangle "**registration**\nthe scenario, named in UserService" as R

  rectangle "registration.createPerson" as R1
  rectangle "registration.createAccount" as R2
  rectangle "registration.resetPassword" as R3
  rectangle "registration.assignRole" as R4
  rectangle "registration.login" as R5

  rectangle "client span\nPOST person-service /api/v1/persons/registration" as H1
  rectangle "client span\nPOST Keycloak /token  client_credentials\n(only when the cached token is stale)" as H2
  rectangle "client span\nPOST Keycloak /admin/.../users" as H3
  rectangle "client span\nPUT Keycloak /admin/.../reset-password" as H4
  rectangle "client spans\nGET + POST Keycloak role-mappings" as H5
  rectangle "client span\nPOST Keycloak /token  password" as H6
}

S0 --> R
R --> R1
R --> R2
R --> R3
R --> R4
R --> R5
R1 --> H1
R2 --> H2
R2 --> H3
R3 --> H4
R4 --> H5
R5 --> H6

note right of S0
  **trace** = the whole request, start to finish.
  **span**  = one step inside it.

  Every span carries three things:
    traceId  - the same across all of them
    spanId   - its own
    parentId - who it hangs under

  The tree is built from parentId alone. Tempo
  stores spans as they arrive, in any order.

  The step spans come from util/Spans:
  mono.name(step).tap(Micrometer.observation(...)).
  Open on subscribe, close when the Mono ends,
  so the HTTP client spans land inside them.
end note

note bottom of T
  This is the only view that shows what one
  registration really costs - and which call took
  the time.

  The service token is fetched for EVERY admin call
  and never cached, so there are more calls than
  steps:
    1 person-service
    2 token + create the account
    2 token + set the password
    4 token + read the role, token + map the role
    1 login
  Ten calls outwards. The tree makes that obvious;
  the code does not.

  Each span also carries the outcome, so a failed
  call is red in the tree while the request that
  contained it may still have answered 201.
end note

note as N1
  The same traceId is in every log line of this
  request - that is what makes one click in Grafana
  go from a span to its log records and back.
end note

@enduml
